1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,670
Have you ever used Linux?

2
00:00:02,992 --> 00:00:08,162
If you're a programmer, maybe the answer
is obviously yes. But what if you're not?

3
00:00:08,957 --> 00:00:10,367
Maybe you use Windows.

4
00:00:11,197 --> 00:00:12,547
Maybe you have an iPhone.

5
00:00:13,237 --> 00:00:15,147
Maybe you've never even seen Linux.

6
00:00:16,118 --> 00:00:20,708
And if someone asked you what Linux is,
you might imagine something like this.

7
00:00:21,419 --> 00:00:23,379
A black terminal. Lots of text.

8
00:00:23,459 --> 00:00:25,329
Someone typing commands.

9
00:00:26,275 --> 00:00:30,175
Linux has a reputation for
being complicated, weird, and

10
00:00:30,255 --> 00:00:34,115
mostly useful to programmers,
system administrators, and people

11
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:37,585
who apparently enjoy making
their computers harder to use.

12
00:00:38,317 --> 00:00:40,267
But here's the interesting question.

13
00:00:41,144 --> 00:00:43,114
Where does Linux actually run?

14
00:00:43,949 --> 00:00:45,919
You might be surprised by the answer.

15
00:00:48,409 --> 00:00:52,599
And to understand why, we need to
go back to nineteen ninety-one.

16
00:00:53,049 --> 00:00:57,029
In nineteen ninety-one, a
twenty-one-year-old Finnish student named

17
00:00:57,109 --> 00:01:01,279
Linus Torvalds started working on
a small operating-system kernel.

18
00:01:02,084 --> 00:01:04,254
It wasn't supposed to become a revolution.

19
00:01:05,224 --> 00:01:07,174
It was basically a hobby project.

20
00:01:08,113 --> 00:01:12,233
Linus wanted to create a Unix-like
system for his computer, and he

21
00:01:12,313 --> 00:01:16,043
released the source code so other
people could experiment with it.

22
00:01:16,721 --> 00:01:19,091
More and more programmers
started contributing.

23
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:22,321
People improved it, fixed bugs, added

24
00:01:22,401 --> 00:01:24,911
features, and adapted it
to different hardware.

25
00:01:25,864 --> 00:01:28,134
And something rather unusual happened.

26
00:01:29,044 --> 00:01:32,434
A project started by one student
became a global collaboration.

27
00:01:33,663 --> 00:01:36,683
Decades later, Linux is
developed by thousands of

28
00:01:36,763 --> 00:01:40,233
people, and supported by
companies, universities,

29
00:01:40,483 --> 00:01:43,513
independent developers and
volunteers around the world.

30
00:01:44,213 --> 00:01:47,953
The most recent release of the
kernel took about nine weeks, and it

31
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:51,523
brought in the work of two thousand
six hundred and fifty-two developers.

32
00:01:52,479 --> 00:01:54,279
It has become one of the
largest collaborative

33
00:01:54,359 --> 00:01:57,429
software projects
humanity has ever created.

34
00:02:00,369 --> 00:02:01,979
And remember that question?

35
00:02:02,961 --> 00:02:04,931
Where does Linux actually run?

36
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:09,437
Well, the answer starts with
understanding what Linux actually is.

37
00:02:10,207 --> 00:02:12,277
And here's the really important part.

38
00:02:13,097 --> 00:02:15,927
Linux isn't really one operating system.

39
00:02:16,713 --> 00:02:18,183
Linux is a kernel.

40
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:21,451
Think of the kernel as
the part of the system

41
00:02:21,531 --> 00:02:23,861
that sits between software and hardware.

42
00:02:24,891 --> 00:02:28,241
It manages the processors,
the memory and the devices.

43
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:34,310
Around that kernel, you can build very
different systems. A desktop computer.

44
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:39,090
A server. A tiny embedded
device. A supercomputer.

45
00:02:40,051 --> 00:02:42,621
And things that don't look
like computers at all.

46
00:02:45,031 --> 00:02:47,461
Which brings us back to
our original question.

47
00:02:48,331 --> 00:02:50,001
Where is Linux actually running?

48
00:02:50,825 --> 00:02:53,195
And now we can finally answer it.

49
00:02:53,908 --> 00:02:56,898
Let's start with something
you probably have right now.

50
00:02:57,631 --> 00:02:58,831
Your phone.

51
00:02:59,881 --> 00:03:03,291
If you're using Android, you're
already using the Linux kernel.

52
00:03:04,095 --> 00:03:06,255
And there are more than
three billion active Android

53
00:03:06,335 --> 00:03:09,645
devices, in over a hundred
and ninety countries.

54
00:03:10,527 --> 00:03:14,537
Android isn't simply Linux in the
same sense as Ubuntu or Fedora.

55
00:03:14,827 --> 00:03:18,177
But underneath Android
is the Linux kernel.

56
00:03:18,945 --> 00:03:21,235
So that little computer in your pocket?

57
00:03:22,134 --> 00:03:23,700
Linux.

58
00:03:25,550 --> 00:03:27,080
And that's just the beginning.

59
00:03:28,057 --> 00:03:29,447
Look around your home.

60
00:03:30,157 --> 00:03:31,777
There's probably another computer nearby

61
00:03:31,857 --> 00:03:34,467
that you don't think of
as a computer at all.

62
00:03:35,195 --> 00:03:36,505
Your Wi-Fi router.

63
00:03:37,531 --> 00:03:40,271
That little plastic box
connecting your devices to the

64
00:03:40,351 --> 00:03:44,001
Internet can contain a
surprisingly powerful computer.

65
00:03:45,111 --> 00:03:46,721
And many routers run Linux.

66
00:03:47,655 --> 00:03:50,485
You don't see the terminal.
You don't even know it's there.

67
00:03:51,175 --> 00:03:52,435
You just connect to Wi-Fi.

68
00:03:52,515 --> 00:03:54,245
But underneath the interface...

69
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:56,786
Linux.

70
00:03:58,636 --> 00:04:00,046
What about your television?

71
00:04:00,856 --> 00:04:03,596
Modern smart televisions aren't
really televisions anymore.

72
00:04:03,676 --> 00:04:06,926
They're computers with a screen attached.

73
00:04:07,995 --> 00:04:09,675
And Linux is used in many smart

74
00:04:09,755 --> 00:04:12,445
televisions and other
consumer electronics.

75
00:04:13,339 --> 00:04:16,599
So you can sit on your couch,
turn on Netflix, and never

76
00:04:16,679 --> 00:04:19,779
realise that Linux may be involved
in several different places

77
00:04:19,859 --> 00:04:22,989
between your remote control
and the film you're watching.

78
00:04:25,739 --> 00:04:27,969
But now let's make the jump much bigger.

79
00:04:28,843 --> 00:04:30,043
The Internet.

80
00:04:30,814 --> 00:04:32,694
When you open a website,
you're usually not

81
00:04:32,774 --> 00:04:35,424
connecting to some magical
place called the cloud.

82
00:04:36,114 --> 00:04:39,254
You're talking to computers.
Lots of computers.

83
00:04:39,334 --> 00:04:41,384
Servers in data centres.

84
00:04:42,818 --> 00:04:48,348
And Linux has become enormously important
in server infrastructure. Web servers.

85
00:04:48,938 --> 00:04:50,608
Databases. Containers.

86
00:04:51,622 --> 00:04:54,062
So when you browse the
web, there's a good chance

87
00:04:54,142 --> 00:04:57,072
you're interacting with
Linux without ever seeing it.

88
00:04:57,734 --> 00:05:00,244
And this is where things
start getting strange.

89
00:05:01,158 --> 00:05:03,988
Linux isn't just sitting
inside a few devices.

90
00:05:04,938 --> 00:05:08,508
It is part of the infrastructure
underneath much of the modern Internet.

91
00:05:11,208 --> 00:05:13,718
Now let's go to the
opposite end of the scale.

92
00:05:14,589 --> 00:05:15,789
Supercomputers.

93
00:05:16,659 --> 00:05:20,389
These are machines built to perform
enormous amounts of computation.

94
00:05:21,439 --> 00:05:23,759
Weather simulations. Astronomy.

95
00:05:23,839 --> 00:05:26,082
Climate modelling.

96
00:05:26,532 --> 00:05:28,992
Twice a year, someone
publishes a list of the five

97
00:05:29,072 --> 00:05:32,202
hundred most powerful
supercomputers in the world.

98
00:05:32,943 --> 00:05:35,033
All five hundred of them run Linux.

99
00:05:35,983 --> 00:05:40,450
And that has been true on every single
list since two thousand and seventeen.

100
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:43,610
Think about that contrast.

101
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:48,860
The same kernel that can be running
inside a tiny embedded computer can

102
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:52,570
also be running on machines containing
enormous numbers of processors.

103
00:05:53,769 --> 00:05:57,389
A hobby project started by a
Finnish student ended up powering

104
00:05:57,469 --> 00:06:00,879
some of the most powerful
computers humanity has ever built.

105
00:06:03,519 --> 00:06:05,889
And Linux doesn't only live in buildings.

106
00:06:06,539 --> 00:06:08,429
It can be found in things that move.

107
00:06:09,334 --> 00:06:12,354
Cars run Linux in various
systems, particularly

108
00:06:12,434 --> 00:06:14,984
infotainment and connected-car platforms.

109
00:06:15,958 --> 00:06:17,678
It's used in industrial equipment.

110
00:06:17,758 --> 00:06:19,778
Robotics. Drones.

111
00:06:19,858 --> 00:06:23,548
Cameras. Storage devices.
Network equipment.

112
00:06:23,858 --> 00:06:25,828
And countless other embedded systems.

113
00:06:26,767 --> 00:06:29,777
The important thing is that you
often don't know it's there.

114
00:06:30,487 --> 00:06:33,457
Because Linux doesn't have to
look like a computer at all.

115
00:06:34,183 --> 00:06:35,873
There doesn't have to be a desktop.

116
00:06:36,243 --> 00:06:38,293
There doesn't have to be a terminal.

117
00:06:38,723 --> 00:06:40,853
There doesn't even have to be a screen.

118
00:06:41,707 --> 00:06:43,827
It can just quietly sit there, running the

119
00:06:43,907 --> 00:06:46,237
software that makes a
piece of hardware work.

120
00:06:49,147 --> 00:06:50,477
And now let's leave Earth.

121
00:06:51,717 --> 00:06:53,907
Linux has even made it into space.

122
00:06:54,817 --> 00:06:58,247
It has been used on spacecraft
and space-related systems.

123
00:06:59,111 --> 00:07:02,251
On the International Space
Station, the laptops the crew

124
00:07:02,331 --> 00:07:05,648
works on were moved to Linux
back in twenty thirteen.

125
00:07:06,098 --> 00:07:09,108
And perhaps the most
entertaining example is Mars.

126
00:07:10,334 --> 00:07:12,494
NASA's Ingenuity
helicopter, which operated

127
00:07:12,574 --> 00:07:15,904
on Mars, used a Linux-based
flight computer.

128
00:07:16,850 --> 00:07:19,200
It was designed to make five test flights.

129
00:07:20,146 --> 00:07:21,346
It made seventy-two.

130
00:07:24,189 --> 00:07:26,139
Think about how bizarre that is.

131
00:07:27,129 --> 00:07:29,429
A piece of software that
started as a hobby project

132
00:07:29,509 --> 00:07:32,139
on Earth ended up running
on another planet.

133
00:07:33,033 --> 00:07:34,703
So, technically...

134
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:36,910
Linux has run on Earth.

135
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:38,920
And...

136
00:07:39,349 --> 00:07:41,059
Linux has run on Mars.

137
00:07:42,005 --> 00:07:44,915
Which gives Linux a
rather unusual statistic.

138
00:07:45,845 --> 00:07:47,735
The number of planets it has run on.

139
00:07:48,682 --> 00:07:49,952
Two. Earth and Mars.

140
00:07:50,946 --> 00:07:52,636
Not bad for a hobby project.

141
00:07:55,526 --> 00:07:58,826
At this point, it might be
tempting to say: Okay, so

142
00:07:58,906 --> 00:08:01,696
Linux is just an operating
system that's everywhere.

143
00:08:02,577 --> 00:08:04,107
But that's not quite right.

144
00:08:05,083 --> 00:08:09,913
Linux is better thought of as a piece
of infrastructure. A building block.

145
00:08:10,813 --> 00:08:14,783
That's why Linux can appear in places
that seem completely unrelated.

146
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:18,500
And probably dozens of
devices around you that you

147
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:21,370
never thought contained an
operating system at all.

148
00:08:24,140 --> 00:08:25,730
So... do you use Linux?

149
00:08:26,685 --> 00:08:29,675
Maybe you don't have Linux
installed on your computer.

150
00:08:30,425 --> 00:08:32,655
Maybe you've never
opened a Linux terminal.

151
00:08:33,385 --> 00:08:35,175
Maybe you don't even like Linux.

152
00:08:36,129 --> 00:08:40,499
But there's a pretty good chance
you've already used it. Possibly today.

153
00:08:41,169 --> 00:08:42,739
Possibly dozens of times.

154
00:08:43,674 --> 00:08:46,104
And that's what makes Linux so unusual.

155
00:08:46,974 --> 00:08:50,684
It started as a small project by
a university student in Finland.

156
00:08:51,623 --> 00:08:55,393
And somehow, it became part of the
infrastructure of the modern world.

157
00:08:56,236 --> 00:08:58,426
From smartphones to supercomputers.

158
00:08:59,388 --> 00:09:00,588
From Earth...

159
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:02,400
to Mars.

160
00:09:04,391 --> 00:09:07,061
And perhaps that's the
strangest thing about Linux.

161
00:09:07,791 --> 00:09:09,801
You don't have to choose to use it.

162
00:09:10,462 --> 00:09:12,532
Sometimes, you're already using it.
